# Helmsman Standards

A green linter is not the standard.

## Reuse before writing

Search first. Stop at the first rung that holds: (1) need it at all? (2) existing code? (3) platform or standard library? (4) installed dependency? (5) smallest inline change? (6) only then new code.

Before writing, name every place the change must reach: callers, tests, fixtures, config, exports. Finish those. A bug fix greps every caller and fixes the root cause once in the shared code. Code you move or merge keeps its validation and its error handling. Between two changes of equal size, take the one that is right on the edge.

No speculative parameters, layers, abstractions, or dependencies. Delete over add; fewest files. Glossaries define language and schema, not which function to call. Never skip trust-boundary validation, data-loss prevention, security, accessibility, or requested work.

## One job

One stated job per module, service, or helper. Extract only when a second caller would paste it or it is a real named concept. One caller: leave it inline. Do not import sibling internals; call the owner.

Length and complexity limits are backstops. A split only to silence the linter fails: delete branches and reuse first. Numeric limits live in the language pack for the file you are editing.

## Impossible states

If a combination cannot happen, the type rejects it. Variant data lives on the variant. A boolean that restates status is a second source of truth. An empty string, zero, and a negative placeholder are not missing values. Parse untrusted input once; downstream takes the parsed type.

Types do not catch a wrong formula in a pure function. That is a protected test on the pure unit.

## Pure domain

Domain functions have no I/O, clock, randomness, logging, or hidden globals; pass time and ids in. Pure logic stays in the domain core. Persistence stays in repositories. Entry points stay thin. Parse with the schemas and data-transfer types that already exist. Dependencies point inward: domain does not import applications, UI, or repo-root `scripts/`. Applications and libraries never import repo-root `scripts/`.

## Delivery and checks

A change ships and reverts alone. Contracts change by addition; never reuse a field's meaning. Persisted string identifiers (wire fields, key prefixes, enum values, index fields) are data: renaming one is a migration. Commands and event handlers are idempotent. Handle every asynchronous result. Do not map a failure onto success, an empty list, or a false success. Empty means there was nothing to return. Prefer the standard library and existing dependencies. A transitive advisory is fixed by bumping the direct dependency that owns it. A root override is only when there is no upstream patch or the bump breaks the build, and only in the workspace root manifest. Intentional shortcuts use `shortcut:` with the ceiling and when to remove it (`shortcut: <ceiling>, <when to remove it>`). An older `ponytail:` comment is the same deferral.

On added lines only: no new warnings, no focused or skipped tests, no coverage ignores, no lint or type suppressions. A suppression copied from another file still fails. Unchanged lines stay. When `.helmsman/tools/checks/diff-guard.sh` is installed, pre-commit and CI run it. When it is not, the same bar still holds and the repo's own hook is the evidence. CI also fails new linter warnings. A package may carry a frozen warning budget above the default. Lower that number when warnings drop. Do not raise one. Agents never bypass hooks (`--no-verify`, or an environment switch that disables them). A hook failure needs a new commit.

## Tests

Test choice selects the test layer: watchable end-to-end, integration, or pure unit. End-to-end is three facts: a person can run a journey locally, a critical-path smoke can fail the pull request, and the exhaustive suite runs after deploy, on main, or nightly. Only the pre-merge smoke gates the merge among those three. A path filter or a `run-e2e` label counts when that smoke can still fail the pull request. The exhaustive suite does not replace the smoke. A pure unit test (unit) does not stand in for the journey.

A native command is enough: `pnpm test:e2e`, Playwright, Cypress, Maestro, or the pull-request smoke job. A missing `.helmsman/setup.md`, `Watch` or `Integration` line, `e2e-smoke.sh`, `integration.sh`, or `languages.sh` does not block feature work and does not require helmsman-setup. Those names are the adapter when a full install runs them. When a native journey already exists, use it. Do not add a second runner. Web and mobile each need a watchable suite when that surface exists. Name which of the three facts is present, and continue. Do not stop the change solely because Helmsman init was never run. When `.helmsman/tools/checks/setup-check.sh` is already installed and the hook runs it, that script remains the adapter for a recorded command. It is not a reason to create the file in a repo that has not installed Helmsman.

Integration (integration) shows code and infrastructure working together. It is not a start gate. Pure unit tests cover pure functions only, completely. Side-effecting code does not get a pure unit test.

A passing test is a protected test. Once a protected test is tied, keep it. New behavior gets a new test. Retie one only when the user has allowed it in this conversation, and say why the old assertion is now false. Procedure when a test or an end-to-end file is open: `helmsman-tests.mdc`.

## Toolchain

Before changing a toolchain version, resolve the publisher's current latest stable in this session. Use that provider's index: the npm registry dist-tag `latest` (and `latest-12` for the pnpm 12 line), the Node release index, or the equivalent for another language. Do not invent a version from memory or from what is most used. `.helmsman/tools/checks/toolchain-latest.sh` fails the commit when a Helmsman-owned Node, TypeScript, `@types/node`, or pnpm version is not that latest stable. A dependency's published peer range can cap the TypeScript version; the gate then requires the newest stable release inside that range.

## Infrastructure and verification

Infrastructure changes only through the repo's infrastructure-as-code. Plan and apply on a non-production environment first. Import existing drift. Do not recreate it by hand.

Confirm library APIs against installed versions. Done means what ran, not what was attempted. A report names each command that ran and its result, and matches the files that changed. A step that did not run is reported as not run, never as passed. Do not claim a command passed when it did not run. Do not claim the task is done while skipping a required protected test. Never hand-edit a lockfile or a generated file; regenerate it. Change only the files the task needs. Never delete or overwrite a file the task did not name. Refactors preserve named invariants. Deterministic checks are authoritative; review does not replace them. High-risk production, data, or infrastructure changes need human approval, a rollback, and post-deploy verification.

## Communication and authority

Comments explain why. State uncertainty. Name what you did not check, and the risk that leaves. Commits use `type(scope): description`. A new feature-flag key needs a tracked ticket before it ships.

Authority: current code, schemas, and the language's own tooling, then `.cursor/rules/`. ADRs record decisions. Plans and backlogs are not standards. Conflicts stop the work.

